// The words of germanium's heat-camera room ("How can a camera see heat?"), in English, European
// Portuguese, Polish and German, kid (Explorer) and sci (Scientist). The picture's own story stays in
// src/content/boards/Ge.js (the easel shows it); the room shows it happening and adds to it.
// Facts: everything radiates by its temperature; Wien's law, peak wavelength = 2898 micrometre kelvin / T
// (293 K 9.9, 310 K 9.3, 373 K 7.8, 1273 K 2.3 micrometres); thermal imagers see 8 to 14 micrometres (the
// air's clear window, where things near body temperature shine most) with an uncooled microbolometer behind
// a germanium lens; smoke's specks (0.1 to 1 micrometre) scatter visible light strongly and long infrared
// much less; ordinary glass blocks infrared beyond a few micrometres; germanium (band gap 0.67 eV, so
// opaque to visible light and to infrared shorter than about 1.8 micrometres) passes about 2 to 14
// micrometres, its refractive index about 4.0 reflects (n - 1)^2 / (n + 1)^2 = 36 % at a bare face, so
// lenses are coated; germanium's transmission falls above about 100 C and it is nearly opaque by about
// 200 C (free carriers; manufacturers' data sheets give these round figures); the Draper point, where a hot
// body first glows dull red, is about 525 C (798 K). The temperatures of the room's things, the kettle and
// the dummy are illustrative; the dummy is a drill dummy with a heater, an invented detail.
